\begin{abstract}
\texttt{tex.web} transformed into {\Csharp}; not another port, rather the {\WEB} is preserved in IDE-friendly {\Csharp}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

I've transformed \texttt{tex.web} into {\Csharp} (and {\Cplusplus}).  Note the wording; this is not another port
of {\TeX} to \emph{some language}.  Rather, \texttt{tex.cs} follows the presentation of the {\WEB} while still
being ordinary IDE-friendly {\Csharp}.  The result is a version of {\TeX} that is structurally faithful to \texttt{tex.web},
usable in a modern IDE, and passes the TRIP test---without using \texttt{tangle}.

\section{Why and why now?}

The `why now?' is easy: Knuth was born in 1938; regrettably, that means we're likely getting close to
{\TeX} version \verb|$\pi$|~\cite{Knuth:TB11-4-489}.  {\TeX} is extremely stable, \texttt{tex.web} isn't going to change much;
so its transformation into {\Csharp} (or {\Cplusplus}) can be done manually once without fear of a moving target.
Should we be fortunate enough to get {\TeX} 3.1415926539 (currently scheduled for 2029~\cite{Knuth:2021:TT})
any changes to \texttt{tex.web} can readily be incorporated `by hand.'

`Why?' is more multi-faceted.  In no particular order, reasons could include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{tex.web} might be one of the more important programming artifacts; but its form
makes it increasingly inaccessible to developers. 

\item Demonstrating that a 40-year-old {\Pas} program can be modernized;
perhaps software engineers want to rewrite a bit too much? 

\item There are few non-trivial programs (other than \MF) which have been published as a book;
it's quite unusual to have such a canonical reference for a program.

\item Knuth once remarked that he was slightly disappointed in the lack of experimentation with {\TeX}
itself~\cite{knuth:questions-and-answers-i}; this, despite clear hooks and suggestions
in the code itself (and even Knuth's own \TeX-\XeT~\cite{Knuth:TB8-1-14}).

\item While function-call overhead is still important, such decisions are usually better left to the compiler rather
than the programmer; especially for a program like {\TeX} which is unlikely to be on a critical hot path.

\item Programming languages now have constructs such as lambdas and reflection which
make it possible to do \texttt{tangle}-like code manipulation in the language itself.

\item The TRIP test helps ensure that the result is truly {\TeX}, not just something that `compiles and runs.'
\end{itemize}

\section{Creating \texttt{tex.cs}}

What follows is a description of the process of creating a readable \texttt{tex.cs} from \texttt{tex.web}.
No tools were created to enable the process to be repeated; there likely isn't much interest in converting
other {\TeX}ware to {\Csharp} in this manner.  (This isn't quite true; I first transformed \texttt{DVIType},
both as a proof-of-concept and for eventual use in running the \texttt{TRIP} test.)

Undoubtedly, the single most important tool used was Martin Ruckert's \texttt{web2w}~\cite{Ruckert:2017:CTW}.
This removed the tedium of munging {\Pas} syntax into C; it also largely implicitly answered the question of
how much \WEB/{\Pas} syntax should be preserved.  For example, using the C-preprocessor,
\verb|help2("a")("b")| preserved while {\Csharp} all but requires \verb|help2("a", "b")|. 
\texttt{web2w} also removes the \texttt{TEX.POOL} file~\cite{Ruckert:2021:WCC}, even C has better support
for strings than Standard {\Pas} did in 1982. Thus, much of the {\Csharp} code is simply the C code from
\texttt{tex.w} rather than hand-ported {\Pas}.

The next step was simple: all {\WEB} modules were turned into comment blocks, and I added
back the original {\Pas} for easy comparison with the {\Csharp} code.  To enable
possible future \texttt{weave}-like processing, each comment block begins with \verb|/*[tex.web]|
and ends with \verb|[tex.web]*/|.
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@* \[1] Introduction.
This is \TeX, a document compiler ...
[tex.web]*/
\end{verbatim}
(This module has an empty {\Pas} part.)  No further attempt was made to 
preserve any other \texttt{weave} features; in particular, cross-reference
and index tags in code were removed or commented-out as they are invalid
code; IDEs have a host of code spelunking features.

For example, at line 948 of \texttt{tex.web} the `\verb|@!|' before \texttt{bypass\_eoln}
was just removed from the \texttt{web2w} transformed code:
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
boolean
input_ln(alpha_file f, boolean bypass_eoln)
\end{verbatim}

Much of the C code produced by \texttt{web2w} can be compiled as {\Csharp} with minimal effort;
that's because  \texttt{tex.w} is a straight-forward transformation of the {\Pas} code, it doesn't use
a lot of C features. We get our first array at line 588, using an \texttt{ArrayOf} utility class, 
the {\Csharp} is
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ The {\TeX} processor converts between ...

@<Glob...@>=
@!xord: array [text_char] of ASCII_code;
	{specifies conversion of input characters}
[tex.web]*/

ArrayOf<ASCII_code> xord = new(text_char);
    // specifies conversion of input characters
\end{verbatim}
which also nicely corresponds to the original {\WEB} code.

Implementing a C-runtime in {\Csharp} targeted at the specific needs of \texttt{tex.w} made
it possible to have the {\Csharp} version passing the TRIP test with minimal changes to
\texttt{tex.w} (i.e., using \texttt{ctangle} to produce \texttt{tex.c}
and then renaming that file to \texttt{tex.cs}).

\texttt{ArrayOf} is one of several pieces of a minimal {\Pas} runtime intended to preserve the original
{\WEB} code rather than convert to language-specific routines; e.g., {\Pas}'s \texttt{reset()} instead
of .NET's \texttt{File.Open()} or C's \texttt{fopen()}.

\section{Preserving the structure of \texttt{tex.web}}
At this point, we have `just' a port of {\TeX} to {\Csharp}; the `C' code in 
\texttt{tex.w} is now actually {\Csharp}.  Yes, this is interesting
and useful (is there an actual {\TeX} implementation in .NET?), but with \texttt{ctangle}
still in the picture it is cumbersome to use in an IDE that is expecting
real {\Csharp} source files.

\subsection{One large class}
The key insight at this point is that both {\Csharp} and {\Cplusplus} (as well as several other languages)
allow most class members to appear in \emph{any order}, a member can be used before it is declared.
By putting all the code into a single class, the effect of \texttt{tangle}'s code-shuffling is
achieved by the compiler.  Doing something similar in C requires forward declarations
which detracts from the {\WEB} presentation.  The result is that \verb|@<Glob...@>=| (line 460)
and \verb|@<Types...@>=| (line 550) just become in-place code:
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ In case somebody has inadvertently made ...

@<Glob...@>=
@!bad:integer; {is some ``constant'' wrong?}
[tex.web]*/

integer bad; /*is some ``constant'' wrong?*/
\end{verbatim}
and
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ Characters of text that have been ...

@<Types...@>=
@!ASCII_code=0..255; {eight-bit numbers}
[tex.web]*/

static readonly Range ASCII_code = 0..255;
	// eight-bit numbers
\end{verbatim}

After that, many of the `C' parts can become member functions as they are standalone
pieces of code which only use `global' variables (which is now member data).  The named
`C' part becomes the name of a member function, replacing spaces
with underscores and removing other characters that can't be part of an identifier.
Yes, this becomes a bit unwieldly in places because {\WEB} treats the names
as \texttt{weave} text with full formatting and potentially long names.
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ We need a special routine to read the ...

@<Report overflow of the input buffer,
	and abort@>=
if format_ident=0 then ...
[tex.web]*/

void
Report_overflow_of_the_input_buffer_and_abort()
{
	if (format_ident is null) ...
}
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Local functions in \Csharp}
The first real complication is when a procedure declares some local variables
which are then used in multiple subsequent parts.  In {\Csharp} this is
handled with local functions~\cite{mslearn:local-functions}. See \texttt{short\_display} at line 3541:
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ Boxes, rules, inserts, whatsits, marks, ...
@p procedure short_display(@!p:integer);
	{prints highlights of list |p|}
    [...]
	@<Print a short indication of the contents
		of node |p|@>;
    [...]
[tex.web]*/

void short_display(integer p)
	/*prints highlights of list |p|*/
{
   [...]
   Print_a_short_indication_of_the__...();

   /*[tex.web]
   @ @<Print a short indication of the contents
   	of node |p|@>=
   [...]
   [tex.web]*/

   void
   Print_a_short_indication_of_the_...()
   {
      [...]
	}
} /*short_display()*/
\end{verbatim}
Notice that \texttt{short\_display()} ends at the second
\verb|/*[tex.web]|\dots\verb|[tex.web]*/| block, after the local function
\texttt{Print\_...()} is defined.

\subsection{Reflection and \texttt{[Module]}}
Some {\WEB} modules are spread throughout \texttt{tex.web}, \texttt{tangle} combines these
into one block of code; initialization is one example.  Using reflection in {\Csharp} (lambdas in {\Cplusplus}),
we can arrange for these disparate blocks
of code to be called all at once (\texttt{[Module]} is a custom attribute~\cite{mslearn:custom-attributes}):
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
[...]
@<Set init...@>=
for i:=0 to @'37 do xchr[i]:=' ';
[tex.web]*/

[Module("Set init...")]
void Set_initial_values_of_key_variables_2_2()
{
	for (i = 0; i <= 31; i++) xchr[i] = ' ';
}
\end{verbatim}

Initialization often takes place immediately after declaration. Both
{\Csharp} and {\Cplusplus} (as well as other languages) allow (`prefer', and sometimes
even `require') initialization to occur at the same time as declaration.  (In {\Csharp} it is
customary to omit initialization that is identical to default initialization).
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@<Glob...@>=
@!deletions_allowed:boolean;
	{is it safe for |error| to call |get_token|?}
[...]
@<Set init...@>=
deletions_allowed:=true; ...
[tex.web]*/

boolean deletions_allowed = true;
	// is it safe for |error| to call |get_token|?
[...]
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Non-local \texttt{goto}s}
Another complication is that many {\WEB} modules contain \texttt{goto}s.
When \texttt{tangle} combines the modules, they are local \texttt{goto}s;
however, as separate functions this is no longer the case. Exceptions can be used 
to emulate these non-local \texttt{goto}s. Although this sounds
bad, it is normally rather structured jumping, usually to a spot near the beginning or end of a
significant block.

The code for catching exceptions is verbose
compared to `nothing' in {\WEB}, but thankfully is rather limited.  Here is the code at
line 1896:
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ @<Get user's advice...@>=
loop@+begin continue: [...]
  end
[tex.web]*/

void Get_users_advice()
{
	while (true)
	{
	@continue: try
		{
			[...]
		}
		catch (GotoException ex) when
			(ex.Label is "continue") { goto @continue; }
	}
}
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Partial classes in \Csharp} 
There are a few functions/procedures in \texttt{tex.web}
that are too large to be presented all at once, some of
them are even spread across multiple starred modules.  Because of other
intervening code, {\Csharp}'s local functions won't work.

The solution is to use a feature unique to {\Csharp}: \texttt{partial} classes~\cite{mslearn:partial-classes}.
This allows the contents of the class to be multiple places in the source
code, each writing \texttt{partial class \emph{name}}, the {\Csharp} compiler
combines these pieces into a single class.  That, together with a helper
\texttt{Ref} class to reference the same member data as the containing
\texttt{TEX} class, make it possible to write the following for the code at line 12319:
\begin{verbatim}[\small]
/*[tex.web]
@ The recursive procedures |hlist_out| ...

procedure hlist_out;
	{output an |hlist_node| box}
label reswitch, move_past, fin_rule, next_p;
var base_line: scaled;
	{the baseline coordinate for this box}
[...]
begin cur_g:=0; ...
[tex.web]*/

void hlist_out() {
	new hlist_out_(this).Invoke(); }
sealed partial class hlist_out_(TEX tex)
	: Ref(tex) // output an |hlist_node| box
{
	scaled base_line;
		/*the baseline coordinate for this box*/
	[...]
	public void Invoke()
	{
		cur_g = 0; ...
\end{verbatim}

