\subsection{Miscellaneous}
As with almost any non-trivial piece of software, reality isn't quite that simple.
There are a large number of minor changes needed to actually get the {\Csharp} code
compiling; for example, several routines in {\S}54
\titleref{System-dependent changes} make strings (and {\TeX}'s string pool)
more natural in {\Csharp}.

\section{What's next?}
Can \texttt{tex.web} be transformed so that it is directly usable in a modern IDE?
Apparently yes, even if the result isn't a good example of idiomatic {\Csharp}
and nobody would write a function name like \texttt{Make\_...()} (very long name truncated). 
Nevertheless, the result remains faithful to \texttt{tex.web}
while remaining ordinary {\Csharp} source code.

It's almost certain that if Knuth were to write {\TeX} today from scratch in {\Csharp} (or {\Cplusplus}),
he would use the dynamic memory facilities provided by the language, i.e., \texttt{new()},
as well as many other standardized features; the result would look nothing like \texttt{tex.cs}.
(Although his more recent work on MMIX for \titleref{The Art of Computer Programming}
shows he still has a penchant for Literate Programming~\cite{knuth:literate-programming},
so he might never give up \texttt{CWEB}).

A similar approach could probably be used for many other languages that support classes
(arbitrary ordering of member names and implicit \texttt{this}) and exceptions
(for non-local \texttt{goto}s).

In any case, whatever would be gained by not using 1982 {\Pas} would surely be lost to PDF, Unicode,
OpenType and numerous other features that are part of today's robust {\TeX} implementations.
Yet, having a canonical {\TeX} available in a modern development environment could enable 
experimentation, education and who knows what else?

